<article class="env-lemma" id="02I7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02I7">Lemma <span data-tag="02I7">5.18.3</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Kolmogorov topological space with a basis of quasi-compact open sets. If $X$ is not Jacobson, then there exists a non-closed point $x \in X$ such that $\{ x\} $ is locally closed. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      As $X$ is not Jacobson there exists a closed set $Z$ and an open set $U$ in $X$ such that $Z \cap U$ is nonempty and does not contain points closed in $X$. As $X$ has a basis of quasi-compact open sets we may replace $U$ by an open quasi-compact neighborhood of a point in $Z\cap U$ and so we may assume that $U$ is quasi-compact open. By Lemma <a href="/tag/005E" data-tag="005E">5.12.8</a>, there exists a point $x \in Z \cap U$ closed in $Z \cap U$, and so $\{ x\} $ is locally closed but not closed in $X$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>